## 📝 Phase 3 — Responsibility Analysis

> Determine what the component IS responsible for and what it is NOT.

### 3.1 — Core Responsibility

```
DERIVE FROM:
  - README purpose statement (if available)
  - Controller/handler endpoint names and method signatures
  - Service class names and their primary methods
  - Domain model names
  - Key business logic class names

ARTICULATE AS:
  "This component is responsible for [VERB] [DOMAIN OBJECT/CONCEPT]
   and returning [OUTPUT] to [CONSUMER]."

Example derivation:
  Controller has @PostMapping("/api/tvs/v1/trades")
  Service has method validateTrade(TradeRequest req)
  → "This component is responsible for validating trade entities
     against business rules and returning validation results via REST API."
```

---

### 3.2 — Explicit Non-Responsibilities (What It Is NOT)

```
FOR EACH of the following dimensions, check presence/absence and state:

  Database / Persistence:
    IF no JPA/Hibernate/ORM dependencies AND no database connection config
    → "Does not own or manage persistent data storage"

  UI / Frontend:
    IF no frontend framework, no HTML templates, no static assets
    → "Does not serve a user interface"

  Message Orchestration:
    IF no Kafka/RabbitMQ producer/consumer
    → "Does not produce or consume async messages"

  Scheduling / Batching:
    IF no @Scheduled, no batch job config, no cron expressions (beyond cache refresh)
    → "Does not perform scheduled batch processing"

  Service Orchestration:
    IF no calls to multiple downstream services being coordinated
    → "Does not orchestrate other services"

  Authentication:
    IF no security config, no JWT/OAuth libraries
    → "Does not handle authentication or authorization directly"
    (Note if delegated to gateway/sidecar)
```

